Lab Context
SQL Injection – UNION Attack: Finding a Column Containing Text    

Overview
In this lab, I explored how UNION-based SQL injection can be used to identify which database columns are capable of storing text data.
Understanding data types is an important step in UNION attacks because the injected query must be compatible with the structure of the original query. Once a text-compatible column is identified, it can potentially be used to display useful information retrieved from the database.

Objectives
The main goal of this lab was to:- Determine the number of columns returned by the original query
- Identify which columns accept text data
- Use a UNION SELECT statement successfully
- Understand how attackers leverage compatible columns to retrieve information

Skills Practiced

Determining Column Count
Before a UNION attack can succeed, the injected query must return the same number of columns as the original query.
This involved testing different query structures until the correct column count was identified.

 Testing Data Types
After identifying the number of columns, I tested individual columns to determine which ones were compatible with text values.
This is a critical step because attempting to place text data into an incompatible column may result in errors or failed queries.

Using UNION SELECT
Once a text-compatible column was found, I used a UNION SELECT statement to retrieve and display data through the application's response.

Key Learning
One of the most important lessons from this lab was understanding that successful UNION-based SQL injection depends on more than finding a vulnerable parameter.
The attacker must also understand:
- The structure of the original query
- The number of columns returned
- The data types expected in each column
Without satisfying these requirements, the database will reject the injected query.

Security Insight
This lab demonstrates why secure database practices are essential.
Improper input validation and insecure query construction can allow attackers to:
- Manipulate SQL queries
- Discover database structure
- Retrieve unauthorized information
- Expose sensitive application data

Defensive measures such as parameterized queries and prepared statements help prevent these attacks.

Reflection
This exercise strengthened my understanding of the logic behind SQL injection rather than simply the payloads themselves.
It showed how attackers systematically analyze query structure, data types, and application responses to move from identifying a vulnerability to successfully extracting information.
Each PortSwigger lab continues to deepen my understanding of how web applications interact with databases and how small weaknesses can lead to significant security risks.


